{"id":"workforce-state-goals","title":"Federal job-training money: the State's goals for San Francisco and what was recorded","question":"For the federal job-training money San Francisco's workforce office passes to nonprofits, what results did the State require, what did it record, and how do other California areas compare?","finding":"The State employment department prints a goal and a result for San Francisco on 43 indicator-years; San Francisco fell short of the goal on 15 (35%). In the year July 2025 to June 2026, 10 of 15 indicators met the goal. The widest miss: Youth: Credential attainment rate, July 2025 to June 2026, 8.3% against a goal of 50.0%.","how":"California's Employment Development Department negotiates yearly goals with each local workforce area for the federal Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act programmes for adults, laid-off workers and youth, and prints each area's result: employment in the second and fourth quarters after leaving, median earnings, credentials and skill gains. San Francisco's goal and result are set side by side and ranked against Alameda County, Oakland, San José-Silicon Valley, Los Angeles City, San Diego and the NOVA area (1 = highest result).","limits":"This is the one workforce result recorded by a body outside the City, but it covers only the federal programme, a small part of what the workforce office spends, and the State does not print results by provider. A result under the negotiated goal is not a failed year in the State's terms: the State judges areas against a level adjusted for the local economy and the people served, which the notices give for earlier years only. Each indicator follows a different group of leavers, youth employment includes going into education, and the latest year's figures can rest on few people.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"indicator","t":"Programme and indicator","type":"text"},{"k":"programYear","t":"Programme year","type":"text"},{"k":"goal","t":"Goal set with the State","type"